 "requested_at": "2026-11-11T15:59:03.723915Z",
 "text": "The clause is enforceable. It restates a rule the Act already provides: a tenancy can be ended by a later mutual agreement between landlord and tenant, and no notice of termination is required. Because the clause is conditional on a future agreement made after the tenancy begins, it does not waive any tenant right under section 4, which bars contracting out of the Act.\n\nSection 37 of the Residential Tenancies Act, 2006 governs termination by agreement. I have not verified the exact wording against the current consolidation, so check it before relying on the citation. Two practical points follow:\n\n- The agreement to end the tenancy has to be a genuine mutual agreement reached at the time. A term that forces a tenant to leave on a landlord's unilateral demand, or that pre-commits the tenant to surrender in advance, would not be covered by this clause and would have to be assessed under the Act's notice and eviction rules.\n- A written, signed agreement is strongly advisable, both to prove the agreement and its date and to avoid disputes over whether the tenant actually agreed.\n\nVerdict: enforceable\nSection: 37",
